import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A2/v0"] = """Problem: In MVFA's few-shot setting, can the K labelled abnormal support images with pixel masks be dropped entirely, adapting the residual adapters per target modality from the K normal support images alone with synthesised lesions (patch pasting and intensity-perturbed blobs whose synthetic masks supervise the Dice + Focal losses), and how much of the image- and pixel-level AUROC of the standard K-normal + K-masked-abnormal protocol does this normal-only variant retain for K in {2, 4, 8, 16}?

Rationale: The released code defines 'K-shot' as K normal plus K abnormal images with masks from BMAD's valid split (G4), so the paper's few-shot numbers assume the expensive part of clinical labelling, while a clinic adopting a new modality usually has only normal studies at hand; the segmentation branch has no other supervision route (G3). The review set shows that normal-only adaptation of CLIP is viable in other frameworks: MediCLIP [10] fine-tunes CLIP with medical anomaly synthesis from few normal images, SpatioPrompt [20] studies the normal-only few-shot setting explicitly, Fed-LoRA [56] and the UWF-fundus adapter [15] synthesise abnormal exemplars, and AnomalyUWF reports strong results from only 32 training pairs. None of these measures what MVFA's multi-level adapters lose when the abnormal support set is removed. The problem changes one input of one stage (the support set of the few-shot fine-tuning), needs only the public code and BMAD, and has a direct measurable outcome: the fraction of K+K performance retained per modality and K, with 3 support draws under the shared protocol."""

A["stage1/problem/B2/v0"] = """Problem: Which image-level pooling of MVFA's anomaly maps (max as in the paper's Eq. 3, mean as in the released training code, top-k mean, or a noisy-OR over patches), applied consistently in training and testing, gives the best and most robust image-level detection, measured by image AUROC and by its degradation when a small fraction of spurious high-scoring pixels is injected into normal test maps?

Rationale: The paper defines the image-level loss with a max over the anomaly map, while the released code mean-pools in training and takes the max of the fused segmentation map at test time on masked datasets (G17, G9), so a single spurious pixel can decide an image's label and the reported results cannot be attributed to the method as described. No robust aggregation is tested, and AUC alone hides how fragile the score is (G11). The related work treats the map-to-image step as a design choice: CANOR [55] replaces naive max/mean aggregation with a calibrated noisy-OR, HeadCLIP [4] builds a joint score from pixel-level evidence, and LaRP-CLIP [17] adds a prototype branch because image scores from global similarity are unreliable. The question is narrow (one pooling operator, used consistently), cheap (the pooling changes one line in training and one in testing, so each variant is a single retraining), and measurable on BMAD: image AUROC per held-out modality, and the drop in AUROC under controlled spurious-pixel injection."""

A["stage1/problem/D2/v0"] = """Problem: Does replacing MVFA's frozen, hand-crafted normal/abnormal prompts (state words averaged over 35 ImageNet templates) with a small set of learnable prompt tokens conditioned on the image's adapted patch features improve zero-shot pixel- and image-level AUROC on a held-out BMAD modality, and does it reduce the sensitivity of MVFA's results to the choice of prompt wording?

Rationale: MVFA assumes one universal text notion of 'normal' and 'abnormal' across modalities and never analyses prompt sensitivity (G5), although the relation between a compact normal concept and diverse abnormal ones is central to anomaly detection (G7). Most recent CLIP detectors in the review set replace static prompts: AnomalyCLIP [11] learns object-agnostic prompts, AdaCLIP [32] combines static and image-specific dynamic prompts, CoPS [41] and VCF-CLIP [22] synthesise prompts from visual prototypes, IQE-CLIP [7] builds instance-aware query embeddings, and prompt-wording studies on medical vision-language models report unstable performance [50]. Their effect has not been isolated inside MVFA's multi-level adapter framework, where prompts interact with every level's alignment loss. The question changes one component (the text side of the alignment) while keeping the visual adapters fixed in design, is trainable on one GPU, and has measurable outcomes: AUROC on held-out modalities and the spread of AUROC across a fixed set of alternative prompt wordings."""
json.dump(A, open("logs/answers/run2/s1_v0_second.json", "w"), indent=1)
